<h2>The setting</h2>
<p>The petitioner, 24, was arrested in May 2026 in a case registered at Chirkunda police station, Dhanbad. Charges framed on 31 August 2026 combined BNS offences &mdash; Sections 333, 109(1), 74 and 75 &mdash; with Section 10 of the POCSO Act (aggravated sexual assault). By the time the bail application reached the High Court, the trial before the Special Judge (POCSO), Dhanbad had begun and two of seven prosecution witnesses had been examined.</p>
<p>The defence&rsquo;s central point was striking: the victim herself, examined in court, had not supported the sexual assault allegation. The State&rsquo;s reply was equally pointed &mdash; the victim was ten years old, and on many aspects she had supported the prosecution case.</p>

<h2>Why the court held back</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The charge-sheet is wider than one count.</strong> The court observed that apart from the sexual assault charge, other allegations also exist against the petitioner. A resiling witness on one count does not dissolve the others.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The trial is live.</strong> Five witnesses remain. Mid-evidence release, especially in a POCSO trial involving a young child, carries witness-influence risk that courts weigh heavily.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The remedy for delay is speed.</strong> Rather than convert delay anxiety into bail, the court directed the State to produce the remaining witnesses promptly and built in a renewal date — April 2027 — if it fails.</div>
</div>

<h2>The calibrated order</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Element</th><th>What the court did</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Bail</td><td>Declined &ldquo;at this stage&rdquo; — language that keeps the door open</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trial expedition</td><td>State directed to ensure prompt production of remaining witnesses</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Accountability</td><td>Order communicated to the district Superintendent of Police and the Director, Prosecution</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Renewal</td><td>Liberty to renew the bail prayer in April 2027 if witnesses are not produced</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Reading it with this week&rsquo;s suspension ruling</h2>
<p>Decided the same day as the Madras High Court&rsquo;s suspension of a twenty-year POCSO sentence where the victim&rsquo;s testimony did not describe the convicted offence, this order completes the picture. At the post-conviction stage, a dispositive mismatch in the victim&rsquo;s account can spring the door open; at the mid-trial stage, the same kind of testimony is one input among several, because the trial court has not yet weighed it and other counts stand. The stage of the proceeding, as much as the evidence, decides what the testimony is worth on a bail application.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the Jharkhand High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and the trial remains pending before the Special Judge (POCSO), Dhanbad.</div>
